# core.autocrlf is on globally on some machines; the generator writes LF and
# tools/verify_format.py compares text, so pin LF in the working copy too.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.lean text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# The record store is bytes, not text. `text=auto` above normalises line endings,
# and these files are hashed: `data/artifacts/<name>` IS its content's sha256, and
# `problems/index.json` carries a digest of every problem document. Normalising
# one byte makes a content-addressed file stop matching its own name and makes a
# clone look like an interrupted save. No artifact contains CRLF today (0 of
# 2654), so this prevents the day one arrives from an upstream file written on
# Windows.
data/** -text
